<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philadelphia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philadelphia is the largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>, on the Delaware river in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a> founded it in 1682 as the capital of his Quaker colony, on land earlier settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a> and Swedes. It was the largest city of colonial America, the meeting place of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/continental-congress">Continental Congress</a>, which adopted the Declaration of Independence there in 1776, and the seat of the Constitutional Convention of 1787.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philadelphia.jpg" alt="Philadelphia" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 颐园居 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Philadelphia_skyline_20240528_(cropped_2-1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Philadelphia lies on the Atlantic Seaboard Fall Line, which separates the Atlantic Plain from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>. The Delaware and Schuylkill rivers are its principal natural bodies of water. The city encompasses about 142.7 square miles, 6 per cent of it water, and its highest point, in Chestnut Hill, is about 446 feet above sea level. The city has been coterminous with its county since 1854.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before Europeans arrived in the early 17th century, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a>, also known as the Delaware Indians, lived in the village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shackamaxon">Shackamaxon</a>, in present-day Philadelphia. Most Lenape were pushed out of the region during the 18th century as the Thirteen Colonies expanded, a loss made worse by intertribal conflicts, newly introduced diseases, mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>, and conflicts with Europeans. In the 1860s the federal government sent most of those remaining in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-territory">Indian Territory</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oklahoma">Oklahoma</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-penn">William Penn</a>, an English Quaker and advocate of religious freedom, founded Philadelphia in 1682, and it became the capital of the colonial Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>. It hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-continental-congress">First Continental Congress</a> in 1774 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-continental-congress">Second Continental Congress</a>, which formed the Continental Army, elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> as its commander and adopted the Declaration of Independence on 4 July 1776. The British Army occupied the city for nine months, from September 1777 to June 1778. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-the-united-states">Constitution of the United States</a> was ratified at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-convention-united-states">Philadelphia Convention</a>. The city served as the national capital on several occasions until 1800, when the new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a> was completed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Philadelphia is the most populous city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> and the sixth most populous in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, with about 1.6 million people in the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Independence National Historical Park, which holds Independence Hall and the Liberty Bell, is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, and the city has 67 National Historic Landmarks. It claims many firsts in the United States, among them the first library (1731), hospital (1751), medical school (1765) and zoo (1874).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philadelphia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Whitefield returned to the colonies in November 1739, preached in <b>Philadelphia</b>, and in 1740 toured New England, where on 12 October he preached to 30,000 people in Boston.</blockquote><a href="/movement/first-great-awakening">First Great Awakening · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Protests followed in Ussuriysk and in Sinhanch’on in Vladivostok, and Koreans in Hawaii and <b>Philadelphia</b> held meetings.</blockquote><a href="/movement/march-first-movement">March First Movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1830s the abolitionist Grimké sisters dissociated themselves from the Quakers when they saw that Black Quakers were segregated in separate pews in the <b>Philadelphia</b> meeting house.</blockquote><a href="/movement/quakers">Quakers · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>William Still, sometimes called the Father of the Underground Railroad, helped hundreds of people escape, as many as 60 a month, sometimes hiding them in his <b>Philadelphia</b> home; he kept careful records and published them in 1872.</blockquote><a href="/movement/underground-railroad">Underground Railroad · People</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1787 Alexander Hamilton told the Constitutional Convention in <b>Philadelphia</b> that the American executive should be an elected monarch serving during “good behaviour”, that is, for life unless impeached, strong enough to resist foreign corruption and yet controlled enough to prevent tyranny at home.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Strengths</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Constitutional Convention in <b>Philadelphia</b> in 1787 the Virginia Plan proposed an executive chosen by the legislature.</blockquote><a href="/government/presidential-democracy">Presidential democracy · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
